# Database design & migrations

Relevant role spec: `.claude/agents/database-architect.md`.

## Split of ownership (ADR-0002 — don't reopen without a named revisit trigger firing)

- **Supabase (Postgres)**: identity/account/session/MFA/invitation/auth-audit data. Schema
  lives in `docs/features/001-authentication/database-design.md` +
  `architecture/database-addendum-001.md`, applied via `backend/migrations/*.sql`.
- **MongoDB**: policies, assets (polymorphic — see
  `docs/features/004-policy-asset-management/database-design.md` for the base-shape +
  type-specific-`details` pattern across all 8 asset types), GPS/location history (`Asset.
  lastLocation`, `location_events` collection — **built and shipped, currently kill-switched**,
  see INC-001 below), claims (not built yet — no `claims.ts` route or collection exists).

## Patterns to reuse, not reinvent

- **Retention/deletion**: never a naive cascade delete across a subject's history. Use the
  `legalHold` flag + scheduled-purge pattern (`app.purge_expired_audit_log()` on the Postgres
  side; the equivalent addendum pattern on the Mongo side) — a vendor- or dashboard-triggered
  cascade destroying an audit trail was flagged as a real risk, not a hypothetical one.
- **Actor vs. subject**: any table logging a privileged action needs separate columns for who
  did it (`actor_account_id` / `actorAccountId`) and who it was done to (`account_id` /
  `targetAccountId`) — collapsing these into one column was a real bug (see migration 031 and
  ADR-0006). Don't add a new audit-shaped table without both from the start.
- **TTL indexes**: reserved for genuinely time-bound, non-evidentiary data — raw GPS pings
  (`location_events`) are the live example, and as of this sync **no TTL index exists on that
  collection yet** (ADR-0009 SDL-8 requires a scheduled, evidenced purge before ingestion may
  ship; it doesn't exist — see `docs/organization/incidents/INC-001-location-ingestion.md`).
  Don't describe location retention as enforced until a purge mechanism is actually scheduled
  and evidenced, not merely coded. Anything that needs to survive as evidence (audit logs,
  access logs) goes on the scheduled-purge + `legalHold` path instead, never a bare TTL index.
- **Cross-store references**: a Mongo document referencing a Supabase account uses `accountId`
  as a soft reference — no FK, the backend enforces existence. Don't design as if a foreign
  key constraint exists across the two stores.

## Migration file convention

New migrations continue the existing sequential numbering in `backend/migrations/`. Check the
latest file's header comment for its stated review status before assuming any migration has
actually been applied to a live database — this repo's convention is "paper design until a
named reviewer signs off," and several migrations have gone from paper to actually-applied
mid-session, so the file's own header is the source of truth, not an assumption based on its
existence.
